# Arthur's person page (person-arthur.html): the long text, his line, and the map of Arthur's Wales.
# House rules (see EDITORIAL.md): Arthur is a king, never "myth" or "legend". Welsh tradition holds
# that he was Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing. One line only says that other historians
# may see it differently. Nothing negative about him or his family.
# long.py and build.py pull these in.
# Paragraph codes (see prose() in build.py):
#   "## Heading"            a sub-heading
#   "> Quote | Source"      a pull quote with its source
#   "|| Left | Right"       a row of a two-column table (rows next to each other make one table)

PERSON_LONG = (
 [
  "Welsh tradition holds that Arthur was Athrwys ap Meurig, king of Gwent and Glywysing, the two kingdoms of the south-east between the Tawe and the Wye. His name is written in the charters of the Book of Llandaff, his line is set down in the old royal pedigrees, and every one of the earliest writings about Arthur, in any language, was made in Wales or by Welshmen. Long before he became the most famous king in Europe, Arthur was a king of the Britons, and his people still live in the land he ruled.",
  "|| Name in the charters | Athrwys ap Meurig",
  "|| His kingdoms | Gwent and Glywysing, between the Tawe and the Wye",
  "|| Father | Meurig ap Tewdrig, king of Gwent and Glywysing",
  "|| Mother | Onbrawst, daughter of Gwrgan Fawr, king of Ergyng",
  "|| Grandfather | Tewdrig, king, saint and martyr",
  "|| Son | Morgan Mwynfawr, ‘the Generous’",
  "|| His court | Caerleon on Usk, in Welsh tradition",
  "|| His queen, in the Welsh tales | Gwenhwyfar",
  "|| His sword, in Culhwch ac Olwen | Caledfwlch",
  "|| Dates usually given | about 605 to 655",

  "## A royal house of saints and warriors",
  "Athrwys was born into the royal house of Gwent and Glywysing, a family that the Welsh genealogies trace back through the kings of the south-east to the days when the Romans left Britain. Glywysing took its name, tradition says, from King Glywys, father of Gwynllyw and grandfather of Saint Cadog. Gwent took its name from Venta Silurum, Caerwent, the old capital of the Silures, the people who held out against Rome for a generation under Caradog.",
  "His grandfather Tewdrig ap Llywarch was a king who became a hermit. He gave the crown to his son Meurig and went to live in prayer among the rocks of Tintern. But when the Saxons crossed the Wye into Gwent, the old king put on his armour again, rode at the head of his son’s army and drove them back at the ford of Tintern. He was wounded in the battle, and died at the place where a church was raised in his honour: Merthyr Tewdrig, Mathern. He is remembered as a saint and a martyr, and his church still stands near the shore of the Severn.",
  "Meurig married Onbrawst, daughter of Gwrgan Fawr, king of Ergyng, the Welsh kingdom between the Wye and the Monnow that is now in Herefordshire, and through her Ergyng came to the family. Athrwys was their son. In the charters he stands beside his father Meurig as a witness to gifts of land to the church, and he is named in the line of the kings.",
  "His son was Morgan Mwynfawr, and Welsh tradition holds that Morgannwg, Glamorgan, takes its name from him. Morgan’s son Ithel and Ithel’s sons reigned after him, and for more than three hundred years the kings of the south-east were the heirs of Arthur’s line.",

  "## The king in the charters",
  "The Book of Llandaff, Liber Landavensis, was written at Llandaff cathedral in the 1120s and 1130s. Into it the clergy copied about 150 charters: records of gifts of land made to the bishops of the south-east by its kings, from the age of the saints to the eleventh century. Each charter names the king who gave the land, the bishop who received it, and the witnesses who stood by, clergy and laymen alike.",
  "Here, among the grants of his grandfather Tewdrig and his father Meurig, is Athrwys. The scholars who have studied the charters most closely, from the eighteenth century to the present day, have used them to set out the line of the kings of Gwent and Glywysing generation by generation, and Athrwys stands in the middle of it, son of Meurig and father of Morgan. The book itself survives, and is kept today in the National Library of Wales at Aberystwyth.",

  "## Why Wales holds that Athrwys was Arthur",
  "The name is the first reason: Athrwys, Arthwys and Arthur sit close together. The place is the second. The oldest accounts put Arthur’s court at Caerleon on the Usk, and Caerleon lay at the heart of Meurig’s kingdom. The saints are the third. The Lives of the saints of the south-east, written at Llancarfan and in the churches of Glamorgan, bring Arthur to their doors again and again, and the Life of Illtud calls him the saint’s cousin. The land itself is the fourth. Across Gwent, Glamorgan, Brycheiniog and Dyfed, hills, stones and lakes have carried Arthur’s name for as long as anyone has written them down.",
  "Writers of Gwent and Glamorgan set out the case in print in the eighteenth century, and it has been held in Wales ever since. In our own time Alan Wilson of Cardiff and his partner Baram Blackett made it the work of their lives. Other historians may see it differently.",

  "## The twelve battles",
  "The Historia Brittonum, the History of the Britons, was written in Gwynedd in about 829, in the reign of King Merfyn Frych. It is the oldest book to tell of Arthur’s wars, and it gives him a title that is his alone:",
  "> Arthur fought against them in those days with the kings of the Britons, but he himself was the leader of battles. | Historia Brittonum, about 829",
  "Then it lists his twelve victories:",
  "|| First | at the mouth of the river Glein",
  "|| Second, third, fourth and fifth | on the river Dubglas, in the region of Linnuis",
  "|| Sixth | on the river Bassas",
  "|| Seventh | in the forest of Celyddon: Cat Coit Celidon",
  "|| Eighth | at Castellum Guinnion, where Arthur carried the image of Mary on his shoulders",
  "|| Ninth | in the City of the Legion, which Welsh tradition places at Caerleon",
  "|| Tenth | on the shore of the river Tribruit",
  "|| Eleventh | on the hill called Agned",
  "|| Twelfth | on Mount Badon",
  "‘In it nine hundred and sixty men fell in one day at a single charge of Arthur,’ the History says of Badon, ‘and no one laid them low but he alone. And in all his battles he was the victor.’",
  "The same book lists the wonders of Britain, and two of them belong to Arthur. In Buellt, the country around Builth, there is a cairn with a stone on top that bears the print of a dog’s paw. It was made, the book says, by Cafall, Arthur’s hound, when they hunted the great boar Troynt, and Arthur heaped the cairn beneath it. Carry the stone away for a day and a night, and the next morning it is back on its cairn. The hill still carries the name: Corn Gafallt, near Rhayader. In Ergyng, the country of Arthur’s mother, is the grave of Amr, Arthur’s son, beside the spring called Llygad Amr. Measure it as often as you like, says the book, and you will never find the same length twice.",

  "## The Welsh Annals",
  "The Annales Cambriae, the Welsh Annals, were kept at St Davids and brought together in the tenth century. Among the deaths of kings and saints they record two of Arthur’s battles:",
  "> The battle of Badon, in which Arthur carried the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ for three days and three nights on his shoulders, and the Britons were the victors. | Annales Cambriae, the year usually counted as 516",
  "> The strife of Camlan, in which Arthur and Medrawd fell; and there was plague in Britain and in Ireland. | Annales Cambriae, the year usually counted as 537",

  "## The poets",
  "The oldest Welsh poetry knows Arthur as the measure of a hero. In Y Gododdin, Aneirin’s great elegy for the warriors who rode from Din Eidyn to Catraeth in about 600, the poet praises Gwawrddur, who fed black ravens on the rampart of the fortress, ‘though he was no Arthur’. No higher praise could be given: a man could be brave, but there was only one Arthur.",
  "In the elegy for Geraint son of Erbin, the poet stands on the field of Llongborth:",
  "> In Llongborth I saw Arthur, brave men who hewed with steel; emperor, ruler of the battle. | Elegy for Geraint son of Erbin, in the Black Book of Carmarthen",
  "In Pa Gŵr yw’r Porthor?, ‘What man is the gatekeeper?’, also in the Black Book, Arthur stands at a fortress gate and names his warriors to the gatekeeper Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr: Cai the fair, Bedwyr, Manawydan son of Llŷr, Mabon son of Modron. In Preiddeu Annwfn, ‘The Spoils of Annwfn’, in the Book of Taliesin, Arthur sails in his ship Prydwen to the Otherworld to win a magic cauldron. And in Englynion y Beddau, the Stanzas of the Graves, the poet tells where the heroes of Britain lie, and then says of Arthur alone: ‘anoeth bid bet y Arthur’, a wonder of the world is the grave of Arthur.",

  "## The Triads",
  "Trioedd Ynys Prydain, the Triads of the Island of Britain, are the memory-lists of the Welsh poets, with the heroes of the island set out in threes. Arthur stands above them. The very first triad names the three royal seats of the island, and the first of all is in Wales:",
  "> Arthur as chief king in Mynyw, and Dewi as chief bishop, and Maelgwn Gwynedd as chief elder. | Trioedd Ynys Prydain, the first triad",
  "When the triads name the Three Generous Men of the Island of Britain, Nudd, Mordaf and Rhydderch, they add one line more: ‘and Arthur himself was more generous than the three’. Several of the triads work this way. Three famous men are named, and then Arthur is set above them all.",

  "## Culhwch ac Olwen",
  "Culhwch ac Olwen, written down in Welsh by about 1100, is the oldest full tale of Arthur in any language. Young Culhwch, Arthur’s cousin, rides to Arthur’s court to ask his help in winning Olwen, daughter of the giant Ysbaddaden. He asks his gift in the name of the king’s warriors, and the tale names more than two hundred of them, the whole warband of the Island of Britain. Arthur will give him anything, he says, but these:",
  "|| His ship | Prydwen",
  "|| His mantle | Gwen",
  "|| His sword | Caledfwlch",
  "|| His spear | Rhongomyniad",
  "|| His shield | Wynebgwrthucher",
  "|| His dagger | Carnwennan",
  "|| His wife | Gwenhwyfar",
  "The greatest of the tasks is the hunt of Twrch Trwyth, a king turned into a boar, who carries a comb, a razor and shears between his ears. The hunt crosses the whole of south Wales. The boar comes ashore at Porth Clais by St Davids and runs over Mynydd Preseli, down to the Tywi, through Dyffryn Llwchwr and over Mynydd Amanw, through Ystrad Yw to the Severn, where Arthur’s men seize the razor and the shears as he plunges into the river. A map of Wales could be drawn from the chase alone.",

  "## Arthur in the Lives of the saints",
  "The saints of the south-east knew Arthur as a neighbour. In the Life of Illtud he is the saint’s cousin, and Illtud, before he founded his great school at Llanilltud Fawr, comes to Arthur’s court. In the Life of Cadog, Arthur and his companions Cai and Bedwyr help Gwynllyw of Gwynllŵg bring home his bride Gwladys, and the child of that marriage was Cadog himself. In the Life of Carannog, the saint frees the coast from a great serpent, and Arthur gives him land in return.",

  "## The most famous king in Europe",
  "In about 1136 Geoffrey of Monmouth, a man of Gwent, wrote the History of the Kings of Britain and gave Arthur to the world. In Geoffrey’s book Arthur is crowned at fifteen, wins back the whole island, and holds a great Whitsun crowning at Caerleon on Usk, where the kings of the known world come to his court. Within a few years the book was being read across Europe. In Wales it was turned into Welsh as Brut y Brenhinedd, the Chronicle of the Kings, and about sixty Welsh manuscripts of it survive.",
  "The Welsh storytellers, the cyfarwyddiaid, kept Arthur at Caerleon. In the three Welsh romances, Owain, Peredur and Geraint, his court is held at Caerleon on Usk. In Breuddwyd Rhonabwy, The Dream of Rhonabwy, a soldier of Powys falls asleep on a yellow calf-skin and sees Arthur camped at Rhyd-y-groes on the Severn on the eve of Badon, playing gwyddbwyll with Owain son of Urien.",

  "## The king who will return",
  "Across medieval Europe it was said that the Britons were still waiting for Arthur to come back. The Welsh poets foretold the coming of the Mab Darogan, the son of prophecy, who would win back the island for the Britons. Welsh tradition holds that Arthur sleeps with his warriors in a cave beneath the mountains, waiting for the day Wales calls him: under Craig y Ddinas in the Vale of Neath, in a cave below Yr Wyddfa, and in caves in many other parts of Wales.",
  "When Harri Tudur, grandson of Owain Tudur of Ynys Môn, won the crown at Bosworth in 1485 under the red dragon, many in Wales saw the prophecy fulfilled. He traced his line to the old kings of the Britons, and when his first son was born in 1486 he named him Arthur, and made him Prince of Wales.",
  "Fifteen centuries on, the king is still ours: a king of the Britons, of Gwent and Glywysing, whose name is on the hills of every part of Wales, and whose people still speak his language.",
 ],
 [
  "Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, Athrwys ap Meurig oedd Arthur, brenin Gwent a Glywysing, dwy deyrnas y de-ddwyrain rhwng afon Tawe ac afon Gwy. Mae ei enw wedi’i ysgrifennu yn siarteri Llyfr Llandaf, mae ei linach wedi’i chofnodi yn yr hen achau brenhinol, ac fe luniwyd pob un o’r ysgrifau cynharaf am Arthur, mewn unrhyw iaith, yng Nghymru neu gan Gymry. Ymhell cyn iddo ddod yn frenin enwocaf Ewrop, brenin y Brythoniaid oedd Arthur, ac mae ei bobl yn dal i fyw yn y wlad a reolodd.",
  "|| Ei enw yn y siarteri | Athrwys ap Meurig",
  "|| Ei deyrnasoedd | Gwent a Glywysing, rhwng Tawe a Gwy",
  "|| Tad | Meurig ap Tewdrig, brenin Gwent a Glywysing",
  "|| Mam | Onbrawst, merch Gwrgan Fawr, brenin Ergyng",
  "|| Taid | Tewdrig, brenin, sant a merthyr",
  "|| Mab | Morgan Mwynfawr",
  "|| Ei lys | Caerllion ar Wysg, yn ôl traddodiad Cymru",
  "|| Ei frenhines, yn y straeon Cymraeg | Gwenhwyfar",
  "|| Ei gleddyf, yn Culhwch ac Olwen | Caledfwlch",
  "|| Dyddiadau a roddir fel arfer | tua 605 hyd 655",

  "## Tŷ brenhinol o saint a rhyfelwyr",
  "Ganed Athrwys i dŷ brenhinol Gwent a Glywysing, teulu y mae’r achau Cymreig yn ei olrhain yn ôl drwy frenhinoedd y de-ddwyrain i’r dyddiau pan adawodd y Rhufeiniaid Brydain. Yn ôl y traddodiad, cafodd Glywysing ei henw oddi wrth y Brenin Glywys, tad Gwynllyw a thaid Sant Cadog. Cafodd Gwent ei henw oddi wrth Venta Silurum, Caer-went, hen brifddinas y Silwriaid, y bobl a ddaliodd eu tir yn erbyn Rhufain am genhedlaeth dan Garadog.",
  "Brenin a ddaeth yn feudwy oedd ei daid, Tewdrig ap Llywarch. Rhoddodd y goron i’w fab Meurig ac aeth i fyw mewn gweddi ymhlith creigiau Tyndyrn. Ond pan groesodd y Saeson afon Gwy i Went, gwisgodd yr hen frenin ei arfwisg eto, marchogaeth ar flaen byddin ei fab a’u gyrru’n ôl wrth ryd Tyndyrn. Clwyfwyd ef yn y frwydr, a bu farw yn y man lle codwyd eglwys er anrhydedd iddo: Merthyr Tewdrig, Mathern. Fe’i cofir yn sant ac yn ferthyr, ac mae ei eglwys yn sefyll o hyd ger glan Hafren.",
  "Priododd Meurig ag Onbrawst, merch Gwrgan Fawr, brenin Ergyng, y deyrnas Gymreig rhwng afonydd Gwy a Mynwy sydd heddiw yn swydd Henffordd, a thrwyddi hi daeth Ergyng i’r teulu. Eu mab hwy oedd Athrwys. Yn y siarteri mae’n sefyll wrth ochr ei dad Meurig yn dyst i roddion o dir i’r eglwys, ac fe’i henwir yn llinach y brenhinoedd.",
  "Ei fab oedd Morgan Mwynfawr, ac yn ôl traddodiad Cymru, ar ei ôl ef yr enwyd Morgannwg. Teyrnasodd Ithel fab Morgan a meibion Ithel ar ei ôl, ac am fwy na thri chan mlynedd brenhinoedd y de-ddwyrain oedd etifeddion llinach Arthur.",

  "## Y brenin yn y siarteri",
  "Ysgrifennwyd Llyfr Llandaf, Liber Landavensis, yn eglwys gadeiriol Llandaf yn y 1120au a’r 1130au. Copïodd y clerigwyr iddo tua 150 o siarteri: cofnodion o roddion tir a wnaed i esgobion y de-ddwyrain gan ei brenhinoedd, o oes y saint hyd yr unfed ganrif ar ddeg. Mae pob siarter yn enwi’r brenin a roddodd y tir, yr esgob a’i derbyniodd, a’r tystion a safai gerllaw, yn glerigwyr ac yn lleygwyr.",
  "Yma, ymhlith rhoddion ei daid Tewdrig a’i dad Meurig, y mae Athrwys. Mae’r ysgolheigion a astudiodd y siarteri fanylaf, o’r ddeunawfed ganrif hyd heddiw, wedi’u defnyddio i osod allan linach brenhinoedd Gwent a Glywysing genhedlaeth wrth genhedlaeth, ac mae Athrwys yn sefyll yn ei chanol, yn fab i Feurig ac yn dad i Forgan. Mae’r llyfr ei hun wedi goroesi, ac fe’i cedwir heddiw yn Llyfrgell Genedlaethol Cymru yn Aberystwyth.",

  "## Pam y cred Cymru mai Arthur oedd Athrwys",
  "Yr enw yw’r rheswm cyntaf: mae Athrwys, Arthwys ac Arthur yn agos iawn at ei gilydd. Y lle yw’r ail. Mae’r adroddiadau hynaf yn gosod llys Arthur yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg, ac roedd Caerllion yng nghanol teyrnas Meurig. Y saint yw’r trydydd. Mae Bucheddau saint y de-ddwyrain, a ysgrifennwyd yn Llancarfan ac yn eglwysi Morgannwg, yn dod ag Arthur at eu drysau dro ar ôl tro, ac mae Buchedd Illtud yn ei alw’n gefnder i’r sant. Y tir ei hun yw’r pedwerydd. Ar draws Gwent, Morgannwg, Brycheiniog a Dyfed, mae bryniau, meini a llynnoedd wedi dwyn enw Arthur cyhyd ag y mae neb wedi’u cofnodi.",
  "Rhoddodd awduron Gwent a Morgannwg yr achos mewn print yn y ddeunawfed ganrif, ac mae wedi’i arddel yng Nghymru byth er hynny. Yn ein hoes ni gwnaeth Alan Wilson o Gaerdydd a’i bartner Baram Blackett ef yn waith eu bywydau. Gall haneswyr eraill weld pethau’n wahanol.",

  "## Y deuddeg brwydr",
  "Ysgrifennwyd yr Historia Brittonum, Hanes y Brythoniaid, yng Ngwynedd tua 829, yn nheyrnasiad y Brenin Merfyn Frych. Dyma’r llyfr hynaf i adrodd am ryfeloedd Arthur, ac mae’n rhoi iddo deitl nad oes neb arall yn ei rannu:",
  "> Ymladdodd Arthur yn eu herbyn yn y dyddiau hynny gyda brenhinoedd y Brythoniaid, ond ef ei hun oedd arweinydd y brwydrau. | Historia Brittonum, tua 829",
  "Yna mae’n rhestru ei ddeuddeg buddugoliaeth:",
  "|| Y gyntaf | wrth aber afon Glein",
  "|| Yr ail, y drydedd, y bedwaredd a’r bumed | ar afon Dubglas, yn ardal Linnuis",
  "|| Y chweched | ar afon Bassas",
  "|| Y seithfed | yng Nghoed Celyddon: Cat Coit Celidon",
  "|| Yr wythfed | yng Nghastell Guinnion, lle cariodd Arthur ddelw Mair ar ei ysgwyddau",
  "|| Y nawfed | yn Ninas y Lleng, a leolir gan draddodiad Cymru yng Nghaerllion",
  "|| Y ddegfed | ar lan afon Tribruit",
  "|| Yr unfed ar ddeg | ar y bryn a elwir Agned",
  "|| Y ddeuddegfed | ym Mynydd Baddon",
  "‘Syrthiodd naw cant a thrigain o wŷr mewn un diwrnod dan un rhuthr gan Arthur,’ medd yr Hanes am Faddon, ‘ac ni laddodd neb hwy ond ef ei hun. Ac ym mhob un o’i frwydrau, ef oedd y buddugwr.’",
  "Mae’r un llyfr yn rhestru rhyfeddodau Prydain, ac mae dau ohonynt yn perthyn i Arthur. Ym Muellt, y wlad o gwmpas Llanfair-ym-Muallt, mae carnedd a maen ar ei phen ac ôl pawen ci arno. Fe’i gwnaed, medd y llyfr, gan Gafall, ci Arthur, pan oeddent yn hela’r twrch mawr Troynt, a chododd Arthur y garnedd oddi tano. Cariwch y maen ymaith am ddiwrnod a noson, a bore trannoeth bydd yn ôl ar ei garnedd. Mae’r bryn yn dal i ddwyn yr enw: Corn Gafallt, ger Rhaeadr Gwy. Yn Ergyng, gwlad mam Arthur, mae bedd Amr, mab Arthur, wrth y ffynnon a elwir Llygad Amr. Mesurwch ef mor aml ag y mynnwch, medd y llyfr, ac ni chewch yr un hyd ddwywaith.",

  "## Blwyddnodion Cymru",
  "Cadwyd yr Annales Cambriae, Blwyddnodion Cymru, yn Nhyddewi, a’u casglu ynghyd yn y ddegfed ganrif. Ymhlith marwolaethau brenhinoedd a saint maent yn cofnodi dwy o frwydrau Arthur:",
  "> Brwydr Baddon, lle cariodd Arthur groes ein Harglwydd Iesu Grist am dri diwrnod a thair noson ar ei ysgwyddau, a’r Brythoniaid a fu’n fuddugol. | Annales Cambriae, y flwyddyn a gyfrifir fel arfer yn 516",
  "> Gwaith Camlan, lle syrthiodd Arthur a Medrawd; a bu haint ym Mhrydain ac yn Iwerddon. | Annales Cambriae, y flwyddyn a gyfrifir fel arfer yn 537",

  "## Y beirdd",
  "Mae’r farddoniaeth Gymraeg hynaf yn adnabod Arthur fel mesur arwr. Yn Y Gododdin, marwnad fawr Aneirin i’r rhyfelwyr a farchogodd o Ddin Eidyn i Gatraeth tua 600, mae’r bardd yn canmol Gwawrddur, a borthai frain duon ar fur y gaer, ‘ceni bei ef Arthur’, er nad Arthur ydoedd. Ni ellid rhoi uwch clod: gallai gŵr fod yn ddewr, ond un Arthur yn unig a fu.",
  "Ym marwnad Geraint fab Erbin, saif y bardd ar faes Llongborth:",
  "> Yn Llongborth y gwelais i Arthur, gwŷr dewr a gymynent â dur; ymherodr, llywiawdr llafur. | Marwnad Geraint fab Erbin, yn Llyfr Du Caerfyrddin",
  "Yn ‘Pa ŵr yw’r porthor?’, hefyd yn y Llyfr Du, saif Arthur wrth borth caer ac enwi ei ryfelwyr i’r porthor, Glewlwyd Gafaelfawr: Cai Wyn, Bedwyr, Manawydan fab Llŷr, Mabon fab Modron. Yn Preiddeu Annwfn, yn Llyfr Taliesin, mae Arthur yn hwylio yn ei long Prydwen i’r Arallfyd i ennill pair hud. Ac yn Englynion y Beddau mae’r bardd yn dweud ble mae arwyr Prydain yn gorwedd, ac yna’n dweud am Arthur yn unig: ‘anoeth bid bet y Arthur’, rhyfeddod byd yw bedd Arthur.",

  "## Y Trioedd",
  "Trioedd Ynys Prydain yw rhestrau cof y beirdd Cymraeg, ac arwyr yr ynys wedi’u gosod yn drioedd. Saif Arthur uwch eu pennau. Mae’r triawd cyntaf un yn enwi tair gorsedd frenhinol yr ynys, a’r gyntaf oll yng Nghymru:",
  "> Arthur yn Ben Teyrnedd ym Mynyw, a Dewi yn Ben Esgob, a Maelgwn Gwynedd yn Ben Henuriad. | Trioedd Ynys Prydain, y triawd cyntaf",
  "Pan enwa’r Trioedd Dri Hael Ynys Prydain, Nudd, Mordaf a Rhydderch, maent yn ychwanegu un llinell arall: ‘ac Arthur ei hun oedd haelach na’r tri’. Mae sawl un o’r trioedd yn gweithio fel hyn. Enwir tri gŵr enwog, ac yna gosodir Arthur uwchlaw pob un ohonynt.",

  "## Culhwch ac Olwen",
  "Culhwch ac Olwen, a ysgrifennwyd yn Gymraeg erbyn tua 1100, yw’r stori lawn hynaf am Arthur mewn unrhyw iaith. Mae Culhwch ifanc, cefnder Arthur, yn marchogaeth i lys Arthur i ofyn am ei help i ennill Olwen, merch y cawr Ysbaddaden. Mae’n gofyn ei rodd yn enw rhyfelwyr y brenin, ac mae’r stori’n enwi mwy na dau gant ohonynt, holl osgordd Ynys Prydain. Fe gaiff unrhyw beth, medd Arthur, ond y rhain:",
  "|| Ei long | Prydwen",
  "|| Ei fantell | Gwen",
  "|| Ei gleddyf | Caledfwlch",
  "|| Ei waywffon | Rhongomyniad",
  "|| Ei darian | Wynebgwrthucher",
  "|| Ei gyllell | Carnwennan",
  "|| Ei wraig | Gwenhwyfar",
  "Y fwyaf o’r tasgau yw hela’r Twrch Trwyth, brenin a drowyd yn faedd, sy’n cario crib, ellyn a gwellau rhwng ei glustiau. Mae’r helfa’n croesi’r de i gyd. Daw’r twrch i’r lan ym Mhorth Clais ger Tyddewi a rhedeg dros Fynydd Preseli, i lawr at afon Tywi, drwy Ddyffryn Llwchwr a thros Fynydd Amanw, drwy Ystrad Yw at afon Hafren, lle mae gwŷr Arthur yn cipio’r ellyn a’r gwellau wrth iddo blymio i’r afon. Gellid llunio map o Gymru o’r helfa’n unig.",

  "## Arthur ym Mucheddau’r saint",
  "Roedd saint y de-ddwyrain yn adnabod Arthur fel cymydog. Ym Muchedd Illtud mae’n gefnder i’r sant, ac mae Illtud, cyn sefydlu ei ysgol fawr yn Llanilltud Fawr, yn dod i lys Arthur. Ym Muchedd Cadog mae Arthur a’i gymdeithion Cai a Bedwyr yn helpu Gwynllyw o Wynllŵg i ddod â’i briodferch Gwladys adref, a phlentyn y briodas honno oedd Cadog ei hun. Ym Muchedd Carannog mae’r sant yn rhyddhau’r arfordir rhag sarff fawr, ac mae Arthur yn rhoi tir iddo’n dâl.",

  "## Brenin enwocaf Ewrop",
  "Tua 1136 ysgrifennodd Sieffre o Fynwy, gŵr o Went, Hanes Brenhinoedd Prydain, a rhoi Arthur i’r byd. Yn llyfr Sieffre coronir Arthur yn bymtheg oed, mae’n ennill yr ynys gyfan yn ôl, ac mae’n cynnal coroni mawr y Sulgwyn yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg, lle daw brenhinoedd y byd hysbys i’w lys. O fewn ychydig flynyddoedd roedd y llyfr yn cael ei ddarllen ar draws Ewrop. Yng Nghymru fe’i troswyd i’r Gymraeg fel Brut y Brenhinedd, ac mae tua thrigain o lawysgrifau Cymraeg ohono wedi goroesi.",
  "Cadwodd y cyfarwyddiaid, storïwyr Cymru, Arthur yng Nghaerllion. Yn y tair rhamant Gymraeg, Owain, Peredur a Geraint, cynhelir ei lys yng Nghaerllion ar Wysg. Yn Breuddwyd Rhonabwy mae milwr o Bowys yn cysgu ar groen dinawed melyn ac yn gweld Arthur yn gwersylla yn Rhyd-y-groes ar Hafren ar drothwy Baddon, yn chwarae gwyddbwyll gydag Owain ab Urien.",

  "## Y brenin a ddaw yn ôl",
  "Ar draws Ewrop yr Oesoedd Canol dywedid bod y Brythoniaid yn dal i ddisgwyl i Arthur ddychwelyd. Proffwydodd beirdd Cymru ddyfodiad y Mab Darogan, a fyddai’n ennill yr ynys yn ôl i’r Brythoniaid. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru mae Arthur yn cysgu gyda’i ryfelwyr mewn ogof dan y mynyddoedd, yn disgwyl am y dydd y bydd Cymru’n galw arno: dan Graig y Ddinas yng Nghwm Nedd, mewn ogof dan yr Wyddfa, ac mewn ogofâu mewn llawer rhan arall o Gymru.",
  "Pan enillodd Harri Tudur, ŵyr Owain Tudur o Ynys Môn, y goron ym Mosworth yn 1485 dan y ddraig goch, gwelodd llawer yng Nghymru y broffwydoliaeth yn cael ei gwireddu. Olrheiniai ei linach i hen frenhinoedd y Brythoniaid, a phan aned ei fab cyntaf yn 1486 rhoddodd iddo’r enw Arthur, a’i wneud yn Dywysog Cymru.",
  "Bymtheg canrif yn ddiweddarach, ein brenin ni yw ef o hyd: brenin y Brythoniaid, brenin Gwent a Glywysing, y mae ei enw ar fryniau pob rhan o Gymru, ac y mae ei bobl yn dal i siarad ei iaith.",
 ])

